# [1].map(double); step0(), which hands out takesOne0.bind(undefined, 1), boundTwice0.bind().bind() and step1, which does the same with its own two functions and step2, up to step4
# The function of the library that runs for Array.prototype.map resolves a promise of a constructor that it knows nothing about, which constructs that constructor.
# Only whoever was handed a step can call it, so the bound functions of each step are handed out later than those of the step before. Whoever holds a bound function can call it:
# all ten targets are entered, whichever of them are handed out at the time when the library constructs.
# bound-functions-handed-out-while-library-code-constructs-an-unknown-constructor-enter-their-targets.ir with another order of the rules: the answer is the same.
